<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Malayan Communist Party</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1930">1930</a>–<a href="/years/1989">1989</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Communist Party of Malaya</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Malayan Communist Party was a communist party founded in 1930, drawing its support mainly from the Chinese of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malaya">Malaya</a>. It led the main resistance to the Japanese occupation and, under Chin Peng, launched an armed revolt in 1948 that the British fought as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malayan-emergency">Malayan Emergency</a> until 1960. Its remaining <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrillas</a> fought a second insurgency along the Thai border until a peace agreement signed at Hat Yai in December 1989.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/malayan-communist-party.jpg" alt="Malayan Communist Party" width="320" height="279" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Original work by Eureka287, vector work by Lasse Havelund, final edit by ComradeBlots · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Communist_Party_of_Vietnam_flag_logo.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Communist youth leagues had appeared among Chinese students in Pahang towns such as Raub and Bentong in the mid-1920s. In April 1930 the South Seas Communist Party was dissolved and replaced by the Communist Party of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malaya">Malaya</a>, which also worked in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thailand">Thailand</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-indies">Dutch East Indies</a>. Illegal under British rule, it was nearly destroyed on 29 April 1930, when a police raid on a house at 24 Nassim Road in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/singapore">Singapore</a> took eight of its founders. It still gained ground in the unions, leading a strike at the Batu Arang coal mine in 1935.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> in 1937 the party drew on anti-Japanese feeling among Malayan Chinese, and by mid-1939 it claimed about 40,000 members. Its Secretary-General from April 1939, Lai Teck, was a British agent. When Japan invaded in December 1941, British instructors trained about 165 cadres in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrilla</a> warfare, and the party's Malayan People's Anti-Japanese Army grew to over 6,000 fighters. In September 1942 the Japanese, informed by Lai Teck, ambushed its leaders at Batu Caves. Lai Teck fled with party funds in 1947, and Chin Peng took his place.</p><p>After the killing of plantation managers in Perak, the government declared an emergency on 16 June 1948 and banned the party on 23 July. Its fighters, renamed the Malayan National Liberation Army in February 1949, reached about 4,000 by 1950, supported by the Min Yuen underground. The resettlement of 500,000 rural Chinese into guarded New Villages under the Briggs Plan cut off their food and shelter, and the Baling Talks of 1955 with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tunku-abdul-rahman">Tunku Abdul Rahman</a> failed when the government refused to legalise the party.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>From the Thai border the party resumed armed struggle in 1969, helped by the Suara Revolusi Malaya radio service broadcasting from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hunan">Hunan</a> until 1981, but purges and splits weakened it in the 1970s. On 2 December 1989 it signed separate peace agreements with Malaysia and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thailand">Thailand</a> at Hat Yai and dissolved the same day. Because of its history, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/communism">communism</a> has remained a taboo political subject in both Malaysia and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/singapore">Singapore</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Malayan Communist Party” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>A second communist insurgency led by the <b>Malayan Communist Party</b> was fought along the Thai border from 1968 until the peace accords signed at Hat Yai in December 1989, and a separate communist insurgency in Sarawak ended in 1990.</blockquote><a href="/empire/malaysia">Malaysia · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Months later the <b>Malayan Communist Party</b> launched an armed revolt, and the government declared the Malayan Emergency.</blockquote><a href="/state/federation-of-malaya">Federation of Malaya</a></li></ul></section>
